<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Kiprono was asked for the locus of points <m>{4}</m> units from the line <m>{y} = {3}</m>. He wrote: the line <m>{y} = {7}</m>.</p><p>(a) What is wrong with his answer?</p><div><ol marker="(1)">
<li>Nothing: Kiprono is right</li>
<li>He has given only one of the two lines</li>
<li>He has used half the distance on each side</li>
<li>He has measured the distance from the axis, not from the line</li>
</ol>  </div><p>(b) Give the correct locus.</p><div>The lines <m>{y} = </m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> and <m>{y} = </m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans3"/>   </div><p>(c) Give the locus of points equidistant from the lines <m>{y} = {3}</m> and <m>{y} = {7}</m>.</p><div><m>{y} = </m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans4"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) The points <m>{4}</m> units from a line lie on both sides of it: Kiprono has given only one of the two lines.</p><p>(b) The locus is a pair of lines parallel to <m>{y} = {3}</m>, one on each side and each <m>{4}</m> units from it: <m>{y} = {3} - {4} = {-1}</m> and <m>{y} = {3} + {4} = {7}</m>.</p><p>(c) Points equidistant from two parallel lines lie on the parallel line halfway between them: <m>{y} = \dfrac{ "3 + 7" }{2} = {5}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>(2)</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>-1</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>7</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>5</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>